# Quantities: functions about a `bytes`, a `cpu` or a `duration` (R-66;
# docs/grammar.md "Quantities and times"). A quantity has operators (`+`,
# `-`, `*` and `/` by an int, `<`); its value in a unit is `quantity.to`.
package quantity

#| The quantity as a whole number of `unit`, written as its literals write it: bytes "Ki", "Mi", "Gi", "Ti", "Pi" or "" for bytes; a cpu "m" or "" for cores; a duration "w", "d", "h", "m", "s", "ms", "us", "ns" (a day as 24 hours) or "mo", "y" for one of whole months. An error for another unit or a quantity that is not a whole number of them.
#| example: let gib = quantity.to(volume.size, "Gi")
fn to(q: any, unit: string) -> int
